[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '77,900 developers started tool that turns any screenshot into working code. Paste an image, get react. And today it\'s sharing the bill with a 1,500 star flight tracker that knows more about your'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'travel history than your airline does, running entirely on your own server. Two open source gems, both free, both demoed right now. First up, screenshot to code, one of the most starred AI tools on all'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of GitHub. The pitch is exactly the name. Feed it a screenshot, a mock-up, a Figma export, even a screen recording of a website in action, and it hands back clean, functional code. There\'s a hosted'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'app at screenshot to code.com if you want zero setup, and the whole thing is MIT licensed if you\'d rather run it locally with your own API keys. It\'s been quietly evolving since late 2023,'

[00:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and the current version is dramatically better than the one that went viral. The category it invented now has a dozen venture-backed competitors, and the open source original is still the one with the community. And the second tool'

[01:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'today? That one\'s the keeper. Stick around. Here it is doing the actual thing. This is the project\'s own demo. A screenshot goes in, the model reads the layout, and structured code comes out'

[01:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the other side. Real components, real styling, ready to edit. The newest versions go further than cloning the shapes. With a Gemini key, it extracts the actual assets, so the real logos and images from your screenshot survive into'

[01:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the generated page instead of gray placeholder boxes. That single feature is the difference between a party trick and a prototyping tool you\'d actually ship from. The numbers back the hype. Nearly 78,000 stars and 9,400 forks make'

[01:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this one of the most forked AI repos in existence. Under the hood, it\'s a React and Vite front end talking to a fast API back end, all MIT licensed. You bring at'

[01:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'least one model key, OpenAI, Anthropic, or Gemini. And with more keys, it automatically mixes stronger model combinations per generation. So, you can compare a Gemini build against a Claude'

[02:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'build of the same screenshot side-by-side. The output options cover most of the modern front-end, HTML with Tailwind or plain CSS, React, Vue, Bootstrap, and Ionic for mobile-flavored'

[02:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'builds. On the model side, it defaults to Gemini 3 Flash and 3.1 Pro, which the maintainer flatly calls the best for this job, alongside GPT 5.5, GPT 5.4 Mini, and Claude Opus 4.6 and 4.8. Video'

[02:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mode is the sleeper feature. Record a site in action, and it reconstructs a functional prototype of the interactions, not just one static frame. That mode needs the Gemini key, and'

[02:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Replicate unlocks image editing and background removal on top. Even the hosted app holds up on a phone. Paste from your camera roll, and the pipeline works the same. In practice, this is where the tool'

[03:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'earns its place. You\'re in a meeting, someone shows a landing page they like, and before the meeting ends, you have a Tailwind version to react to. It won\'t replace a design system, and the maintainer is upfront that local'

[03:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'open-source models produce poor results. The quality lives in the frontier models. But as a zero-to-prototype accelerator, nothing else this free is this fast. And now, the one I promised, the tool I\'m actually keeping installed.'

[03:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This is Airtrail, a modern, self-hosted personal flight tracking system, and easily the prettiest thing I\'ve self-hosted this year. Every flight you\'ve ever taken renders on an'

[03:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'interactive world map with your routes arcing across a globe you can spin from your phone. Underneath the eye candy is a real database, full flight history with search and filters, plus statistics on distance flown, hours in the air,'

[03:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'airports visited, and airlines used. The pitch in one line, map every journey, analyze your travel patterns, and own your data. No third-party app scraping your inbox. Airtrail comes from Johan'

[04:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Olie, a developer in Copenhagen, shipping it in the open under GPL3. It\'s the kind of project this channel exists for. One person, a real itch, wanting a flight log that isn\'t a'

[04:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'subscription app, and 60 public repos worth of practice behind it. The project started in August 2024 and has climbed past 1,500 stars on pure word of mouth in the self-hosting community, with a'

[04:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Docker image pulling steadily on Docker Hub. No company, no pricing page, no telemetry drama, just a maintainer who flies and software that respects that the data is yours. The feature list'

[04:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'reads like someone actually uses their own product. The world map is the centerpiece, but the importers are the killer feature. Airtrail ingests your history from MyFlightRadar24, App in the Air, Jetlag, TripIt, Flighty,'

[04:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'By Air, Jetlovers, and OpenFlights. So, a decade of tracked flights moves over in minutes instead of being retyped. Multi-user support means the whole family logs trips in one place with'

[05:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'proper authentication and your own OAuth provider if you run one. Responsive design, dark mode, the modern self-hosted checklist, fully ticked. Hosting it yourself is a two-command'

[05:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'evening project. Pull the image from Docker Hub. It ships as jahufi/airtrail. Bring it up with compose alongside its database, and the app is live on your local network. From there, you create'

[05:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your admin user, point your OAuth provider at it if you have one, and start importing. If you\'ve self-hosted anything before, the only surprising part is how little configuration a tool'

[05:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this polished asks for. The obvious question, why not just use Flighty or the Flight Radar 24 app? They\'re slicker in places and nobody\'s pretending otherwise, but the trade is straightforward. The commercial apps'

[06:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'live on subscriptions, keep your travel history on their servers, and can sunset features or the whole product whenever the business model wobbles. Air traffic is GPL3, your server, your database,'

[06:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your data, exportable forever, free for every user you add. For something as personal as a lifetime of travel, permanence is the feature. And if the project ever stopped tomorrow, your'

[06:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'instance keeps running and your history stays queryable. That\'s the quiet superpower of self-hosting done right. Both repos are linked below. Prototype your next idea from a screenshot, then map the flights to your next conference'

[06:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on hardware you own. Star them both and subscribe to Prism Labs for the open-source tools worth your weekend.'
